  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 415 AM EDT SAT AUG 8 2026\n\nWith the return of quasi-zonal flow aloft, a weak surface pressure \ngradient, and the increased potential for antecedently wet grounds, \nSunday night is shaping up to be a candidate for nocturnal radiation \nfog. Confidence in fog formation is greatest in the typically-\nimpacted river valleys, but patchier fog may develop elsewhere in \nsuch a setup. Expect fog to begin to mix out during the Monday \nmorning commute, setting up a generally pleasant, but hot, late \nsummer day in eastern Kentucky. Most of the area will experience a \nreprieve from the recent excessive rainfall risks on Monday, with \nonly isolated showers and storms expected along the terrain in SE \nKY. Continued midlevel height rises and strengthening southwesterly \nlow level flow will yield highs in the upper 80s/lower 90s. \nDewpoints in the 70s combine with these temperatures to yield \nwidespread heat indices in the mid to upper 90s. A few readings near \n100 degrees cannot be ruled out in urbanized areas and in the \ndeepest valley locales.  \n\nThe attention then turns towards a series of ridge-riding shortwave \nimpulses approaching the forecast area between Tuesday and Saturday. \nThe presence of these disturbances in the coarse long term guidance \ncauses scattered to numerous coverage-level PoPs to reenter the \nforecast by midweek, but it remains difficult to pinpoint the exact \ntemporal oscillations within those PoPs. Pattern recognition \ncontinues to suggest that next week's convection could take the form \nof MCSes moving through the Ohio River Valley. The available medium-\nrange machine learning models and analog-based convective guidance \nstill suggest that a risk for damaging wind gusts could materialize \nin the late Tuesday to Wednesday time frame. This will likely be \naccompanied by a threat for heavy rainfall, and WPC has already \ndelineated a Slight (Level 2/4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook for \nTuesday. Depending on the evolution of each successive round of \nconvection, a risk for flash flooding could materialize across the \nCommonwealth. Confidence in forecast details like rainfall totals \nand favored corridors for training storms/backbuilding is low at the \ncurrent temporal range, so interests are encouraged to monitor for \nupdates in the coming days. On the plus side, increased sky cover \nshould help quell the heat in the latter stages of the week. Expect \ntemperatures to modulate back down towards seasonably appropriate \n(if not slightly below) highs, in the low to mid 80s, and lows in \nthe 60s by the end of the period on Friday and Saturday.",
